A: The answer here https://stackoverflow.com/a/29625284/
may solve your case if "echo" is your friend, but I have used "sed".
 sed -e 'i\n//2,a7\n//a2\n' file

And I can easily understand why with sed.
You are looking for:


*

*the line containing the text 0  

*a copy of that in the lines from 1 to 10


So the logic is as follows:


*

*the starting line is i//2,a

*the beginning of line 2 is a//a

*the beginning of line 7 is //a2
So the pattern would look like:
 sed -e 's//2,/i//' 

This will move the the line containing 0 to the beginning, then copy i onto itself. This will result in the following:


*

*4 lines 0,1,2,a2 

*5 lines a2,a5,a10

*6 lines i

*7 lines i//a a


So the line containing 0 is as follows: 
     //a2 \n // a5 \n // a10 \n // i\n // .\n //  \n // .. \n // ..
     \n   // //2 , //3, //4, //5 \n   //// 6, //7, a7 \n   ////
     \n   a //a2 \n   // //a5 \n   // //a10 \n   // // i \n   //. \n   // . \n   // . 
     \n   a //a5 \n   // //a10 \n     // i \n     // // /  etc, etc. 

But if you do not want two copies, you can repeat the search to find the second line to put the text of line 0 onto:
sed -e 's//2,//a2'

In this case you will need a replacement of //a2 so you would end up with the following:
sed -e 's//2,/i/a2'

To make sure you have the correct starting line, you can also use the following:
sed -e 's//a2//'

So it would be
 sed -e 's//2,//a2//'

This will move the line containing 0 to the beginning, then copy a2 onto itself. This would have the following output:
    //a2 //a5 //a10 // i // .... // .......//

     //// 7   //   //// . \n // // ... \n // ..
     ///  // //// 6, //7, //a7 \n   ////  \n   // //
     // a//a2   \n
